WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.770
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.730
Using light simulation programs, we are able
to populate a virtual scene with objects,

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.599
assign material models to them, and create
a beautiful, photorealistic image of this

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:15.599
digital scene.

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:20.990
The theory of light transport follows the
physics of light, therefore these images should

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.590
be indistinguishable from reality, and fortunately,
they often are.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.790
However, there are some cases when we can
tell them apart from real images.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:34.109
And the reason for this is not the inaccuracies
of the light transport algorithm, but the

00:00:34.109 --> 00:00:38.460
oversimplifications used in the geometry and
material models.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.260
The main issue is that in our mathematical
models, these materials are often defined

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.469
to be too perfect.

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:50.030
But in reality, metals are rarely perfectly
polished, and the classical material models

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.589
in light transport can rarely capture these
microstructures that make surfaces imperfect.

00:00:55.589 --> 00:01:01.359
This algorithm is about rendering new material
models that can represent the imperfect materials

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:07.190
like scratched coating and metallic flakes
on carpaint, a leather sofa, wooden floor,

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:08.350
or a teapot.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.080
Just look at these phenomenal images.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.830
Previous techniques exist to solve this problem,
but they take extremely long and are typically

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.930
limited to flat surfaces.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.210
One of the main difficulties of the problem
is that these tiny flakes and scratches are

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.240
typically orders of magnitude smaller than
a pixel, and therefore they require a lot

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.220
of care and additional computations to render.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:35.220
This work provides an exact, closed-form solution
to this that is highly efficient to render.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:41.410
It is over a hundred times faster than previous
techniques, has less limitations as it works

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:47.820
on curved surfaces, and, it only takes 40%
longer to render it compared to the standard

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.560
perfect material models.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.950
Only 40% more time for this?

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:52.950
Sign me up!

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:58.410
It is truly incredible that we can create
images of this sophistication using science.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:03.570
It is also highly practical as it can be plugged
in as a standard material model without any

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.450
crazy modifications to the simulation program.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:11.599
Looking forward to seeing some amazing animations
using more and more realistic material models

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:12.709
in the near future.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:17.659
If you would like to learn more about light
simulations, I have been holding a full master-level

00:02:17.659 --> 00:02:21.599
course on it at the Technical University of
Vienna for a few years now.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:26.269
After a while, I got a strong feeling that
the teachings shouldn't only be available

00:02:26.269 --> 00:02:30.780
for the lucky 30 people in the classroom who
can afford a college education.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.659
The teachings should be available for everyone.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:39.030
And now, the entirety of this course is available
free of charge for everyone where we learn

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:44.330
the theory of light from scratch and implement
a really cool light simulation program together.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.640
If you want to solve a few infinite dimensional
integrals with me, give it a go!

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.530
As always, details are available in the video
description.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

